WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
"And it seems like Faker's going to be playing the Taric."

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:03.680
"Really?"

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:04.300
"Yes."

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:05.900
"He's gonna play Taric."

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.440
"In a professional game."

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:08.760
"Okay there it is."

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.580
If it’s been a while since you’ve watched competitive League of Legends,

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.800
you might be asking yourself, what the f*** is going on right now?

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.880
We're over a week into the Summer Split and we've already seen some of the zaniest strategies

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.020
that pro league has seen in years.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:26.600
"Oh boy, Nunu Karthus is coming out on the blue side. They want Nunu Karthus."

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.920
"Oh and we might have a Taric Yi against it."

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:31.080
"We're getting it."

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.060
"It's happening."

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:33.040
"We're popping off in the first game."

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.400
"It's the first game of LCK 2018 Summer."

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.380
"And we're gonna get it right away Karthus gets locked in."

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:45.880
And though it’s still early in the split, this huge boost in creative compositions could be exactly what Riot needs,

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.900
at a critical time for their game.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:57.420
So after seeing a handful of games with admittedly insane team comps,

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.900
the League community has reacted a bit strongly.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.440
But, what's the big deal?

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:07.580
Riot has made changes before, huge changes that have impacted the way League is played.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:12.520
Think of 2015's Juggernaut patch or last year's infamous Ardent Censor meta.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.980
(Casting)

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.360
"Now this is a risky composition to have."

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.580
"They have no real front line."

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:26.320
"They have no any kind of front line, real or imaginary. None of these guys are tanky."

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.500
"You know what they do have Flowers, double Ardent Censor."

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:33.040
But this current meta is a way bigger deal than any of those.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.740
Simply put, these changes are coming at one of the most important moments

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.380
in League of Legends’ history.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.680
League is an old game, a really old game.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:48.980
It first came out in 2009, and it's had a healthy competitive scene since season one in 2010 and 2011.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.320
And though that scene has come a long way,

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:55.500
it's rare for people to stick with a game as long as they've stuck with League of Legends.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.740
There are some older players, who have been playing the game for nearly a decade,

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:03.560
who might be starting to get bored of a game that while not unchanging, is starting to get stale.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:08.860
Many of the game's older guard of content creators  have started to move on to other big games.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:16.100
"It's 2018 and the question is: where are the League videos? And yeah they're not gonna be a main

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:16.920
thing anymore."

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:18.760
"Nice."

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:22.000
For some, that’s been Epic Games’ smash hit, Fortnite.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.820
Fortnite is just the latest in a litany of games that the community has decided is gonna kill League.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.300
Obviously, it's not that simple

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:36.000
League of Legends isn't gonna disappear tomorrow because Ninja got a particularly sweet victory royale

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:41.900
(Stream)

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.500
But we've seen other games have moments like this

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.080
The biggest hits of yesteryear

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.900
from World of Warcraft, StarCraft, to plenty of others

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.800
all saw declining player bases eventually for a number of reasons.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.160
And player dissatisfaction is a big one.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:00.640
So it's fair to say that this period of meta unrest is coming at an important time in League of Legends

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:01.540
history.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.620
And, in North America, this chaotic patch is coming in the middle of the first season of a newly-franchised

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.560
League that saw millions of dollars be poured into the scene.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:15.580
Relegation might not be a factor anymore. But it might be hard to see a reliable return on investment

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:17.380
when the meta's just so unpredictable.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:22.460
Pros and team owners have been complaining about Riot's constant changes to their game for years.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:28.400
In 2016, Team SoloMid’s owner Reginald compared League’s constantly changing format to Basketball.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.260
"You look at the NBA right where you got into the NBA playoffs."

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:35.440
"It would be essentially changing the basketball, basketball's weight.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.160
and changing it into shooting a bowling ball instead of a basketball."

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.220
And don’t kid yourself, meta swings this huge have a serious impact on the pro players.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.880
"It's very scary for a lot of the pro players though because the ones that can't

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:50.760
adapt like that or, feel uncomfortable on other champions like,

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:54.280
your job is at stake. Kind of your livelihood at stake.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:04:00.460
If something like this lasts a long time then maybe  you actually end up having to worry about getting replaced."

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:04.540
Just take a look at some of the most dramatic role swaps we've already seen from the pros.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:10.160
Huni played Yasuo in the bottom lane in Echo Fox’s game against FlyQuest and jungle vs. Clutch Gaming,

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:12.380
forcing Altec and Dardoch to play top.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:22.480
(Casting)

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:27.720
In a game against Afreeca, Faker took Darius to the bottom lane while Bang played Lulu mid.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.640
"This is what happened is that they had to be comfortable

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:37.140
going further flex and that is Faker playing his first ever competitive duo bot lane game as Darius."

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:42.360
Against Gen.G, Faker played Taric mid to funnel farm into Blossom’s Master Yi.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:43.860
He didn’t even CS.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:55.060
(Casting)

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:58.420
Pro players are great at League of Legends, there's no question there.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:02.180
But they are extraordinary polished players in one role.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:06.320
Now sometimes that role requires you to learn multiple types of champion.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:11.360
For example top laners have had to had experience playing both tanks and carries in their careers.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:23.300
But a world in which the best players need to be good at multiple roles. Could look very different from the world

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.520
that most pro players are used to.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.340
And if this unpredictable meta sticks around,

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.880
It could have a huge impact on the game.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:35.100
"You go through the things that Faker has done this season. He's tried to play carry he's

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:39.600
tried to play facilitator, he's gone bot lane, he's gone mid lane, and now he's a support.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.660
They're trying it all Valdes. They don't know the right way to move forward and none of those strategies

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:45.360
have worked.

00:05:45.720 --> 00:06:02.000
(Casting)

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.020
And the meta has been unpredictable.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:09.280
Bruisers going bot lane, hyper farming Master Yi and Karthus and Taric going mid.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:10.740
So how did we get here?

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:12.700
There's a few answers for that.

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:16.900
A big part of the meta shifts came as a part of a bold plan from Riot Games.

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:19.640
They’d planned to work their way through several roles,

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:21.660
significantly reworking each one.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:25.840
In patch 8.10, they drastically changed junglers and jungle experience.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:31.780
In 8.11, they reworked AD Carry items, and reduced base damage for the game’s traditional marksmen.

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:34.680
But there have been big picture changes to the game, too.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:39.180
Recent patches have seen an increase to bounty gold for killing fed enemy champions,

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.260
and a decrease to global gold for the first outer turrets.

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:47.160
This pushes some of the gold available in a game away from traditional objectives like towers

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:51.840
and towards kills. Incentivizing players to kill fed enemy champions.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.260
Now, all these changes on their own might not have impacted the game all that much aside

00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:59.800
from a few champions rising to prominence and a couple dropping down.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:03.880
But, put them all together and they've changed the game.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:05.060
Dramatically.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:20.880
(Casting)

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:25.420
And though some players in the community are very unhappy with the changes…

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:30.960
others are embracing a new meta that's seen players have more freedom in draft then they've had in years.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:34.340
"We've seen it already, the first week of pro play has kind of exploded

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:36.860
and we've definitely had some exciting games already."

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:38.500
"I have been absolutely loving it."

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:43.280
"I think it's really cool that you can do all these different things and have the champions played in different ways.

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:46.060
Even Mordekaiser which has always traditionally been a carry.

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:51.380
Seeing Mordekaiser Shyvana whether or not it works, but the Mordekaiser as the support mid

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:55.620
essentially like playing a different role more supportive role, I think is just super cool."

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:59.520
Riot Games says on their site’s Operations page that their mission is

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:03.020
“making League a global sport that lasts for generations.”

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:10.000
But for that to happen, they need to keep new players coming in,

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:11.860
And while some players are gonna come and go,

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:16.000
they're also gonna need existing players to stick around if they want a large player base.

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:18.620
One way to do that is to keep the game fresh.

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:21.080
Basically, Riot needs new players.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:25.020
Whether Riot intended to make such a drastic change to the way League is played,

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:29.440
the fact that they wanted to significantly rework roles between the Spring and Summer Split

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:33.440
is a sign that Riot wants to keep older players interested and attract new ones.

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:38.700
Maybe they went too far. Riot's already had to cancel their planned Fighter update to deal with all the

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:41.300
the chaos they caused in 8.10 and 8.11.

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:45.020
But there's no question that League feels pretty different right now.

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:48.160
Will this be a shot in the arm for Riot's older players?

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.460
Will this be a flashpoint that pushes the game into its second decade?

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:54.420
Will it be the reason everybody leaves League in droves?

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:55.880
There's no way to know.

00:08:55.880 --> 00:09:00.260
But these are the sort of bigger picture questions that Riot must be thinking about right now,

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:02.600
now that they've turned their game upside down.

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:06.840
The thing is, Riot usually changes things back to normal by now.

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:10.580
But Riot hasn't changed anything about this crazy new meta yet.

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:14.980
In fact, in the patch notes for 8.12. Riot says they're committed to smoothing out the edges

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:17.760
and not disrupting this new meta they've created.

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:19.580
It's still early in the season.

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:24.200
and it’s hard to say whether or not these strategies will stick around as people begin to solve the meta.

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:28.920
But at least for now, we’re seeing a huge wave of different and creative strategies

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:30.540
define League of Legends.

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:33.980
And if Riot is interested in maintaining their game's popularity.

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:37.840
Different and creative, are going to be two very important words.

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:44.060
"So first off, I personally don't like the term "fiesta" for having mages and bruisers and melees

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:48.340
in the bottom lane. And we've also been calling it bot lane for about a year now."

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:48.880
"Yeah."

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:51.280
"In preparation we saw it coming."

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:54.060
"Stronger bot lane carry I think is this question."

